---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Tldr, Eli5, WhyItMatters, HowItWorks, FailureModes, WhatILearned, DeepDive } from '../components/blocks';
import Sources from '../components/Sources.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Password Cracking"
  description="Why some passwords fall in under a second and others hold for centuries — and why the site you signed up on matters more than your password."
>
  <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/security/">← Security</a></p>
  <h1>Password Cracking</h1>

  <div class="depth-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Reading depth">
    <button type="button" class="depth-btn" data-level="0" aria-pressed="true">ELI5</button>
    <button type="button" class="depth-btn" data-level="1">Beginner</button>
    <button type="button" class="depth-btn" data-level="2">Intermediate</button>
    <button type="button" class="depth-btn" data-level="3">Deep Dive</button>
  </div>

  <Tldr>
    Password strength is <strong>half the story</strong>. The other half:
    how the site stored it. A "strong" password hashed badly falls in
    seconds. A plain phrase hashed well holds for centuries.
  </Tldr>

  <Eli5>
    Two locks. One lets you try a new combo every millisecond, forever.
    The other makes you wait a full second between guesses. Lock two is
    less convenient — for everyone, including you — but it's why a
    break-in takes years instead of minutes.
  </Eli5>

  <div data-min-level="1" hidden>
    <WhyItMatters>
      <ul>
        <li>A breach doesn't leak your password. It leaks a <strong>hash</strong> of it.</li>
        <li>Cracking = guess a password → hash the guess → check for a match.</li>
        <li>Speed depends almost entirely on <strong>which hash function the site used</strong> — not on how clever your password felt.</li>
      </ul>
    </WhyItMatters>
  </div>

  <div data-min-level="2" hidden>
    <HowItWorks>
      <p>Two numbers decide crack time:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Entropy</strong> — total guesses that exist for your password.</li>
        <li><strong>Hash speed</strong> — guesses per second an attacker can try.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Hash speed swings by <strong>seven orders of magnitude</strong>
        depending on the algorithm — see the table below. Same leaked
        database, wildly different outcome.
      </p>
    </HowItWorks>
  </div>

  <section class="lab" aria-labelledby="lab-heading">
    <h2 id="lab-heading" class="eyebrow section-heading">Try it yourself</h2>
    <p class="lab-note">
      Runs entirely in your browser. Nothing you type is sent anywhere,
      logged, or stored.
    </p>
    <label for="pw-input" class="pw-label">Type a password:</label>
    <input id="pw-input" type="text" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="try something" />

    <div class="common-flag" id="common-flag" hidden>
      ⚠ This exact password shows up on common leaked-password lists.
      Every cracking attempt tries these <strong>before</strong> any of
      the math below — entropy doesn't matter if the guess is free.
    </div>

    <div class="charset-grid" id="charset-grid" aria-live="polite">
      <div class="cs-cell"><span class="cs-key">Length</span><span class="cs-val" id="cs-length">0</span></div>
      <div class="cs-cell"><span class="cs-key">lowercase</span><span class="cs-val" id="cs-lower">0</span></div>
      <div class="cs-cell"><span class="cs-key">UPPERCASE</span><span class="cs-val" id="cs-upper">0</span></div>
      <div class="cs-cell"><span class="cs-key">digits</span><span class="cs-val" id="cs-digit">0</span></div>
      <div class="cs-cell"><span class="cs-key">symbols</span><span class="cs-val" id="cs-symbol">0</span></div>
      <div class="cs-cell"><span class="cs-key">Entropy</span><span class="cs-val" id="cs-entropy">0 bits</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="hash-table" id="hash-table" aria-live="polite">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Attack scenario</th><th>Guesses / sec</th><th>Est. crack time</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Online, rate-limited login</td><td>10</td><td class="ht-val" data-rate="10">—</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Online, no rate limit</td><td>1,000</td><td class="ht-val" data-rate="1e3">—</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Offline, Argon2id (recommended)</td><td>~100</td><td class="ht-val" data-rate="1e2">—</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Offline, bcrypt (cost 12)</td><td>~2,500</td><td class="ht-val" data-rate="2.5e3">—</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Offline, bcrypt (cost 10)</td><td>~10,000</td><td class="ht-val" data-rate="1e4">—</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Offline, unsalted SHA-256 (GPU)</td><td>~10 billion</td><td class="ht-val" data-rate="1e10">—</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Offline, unsalted MD5 (GPU)</td><td>~100 billion</td><td class="ht-val" data-rate="1e11">—</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div data-min-level="1" hidden>
    <FailureModes>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>"P@ssw0rd1!" isn't strong.</strong> Swapping letters for symbols is the first trick cracking dictionaries try.</li>
        <li><strong>Reused passwords get cracked once, used everywhere.</strong> Your bank password doesn't need cracking if it's the same one from a forum breach three years ago.</li>
        <li><strong>Length beats complexity.</strong> Four random words usually beats an 8-character symbol soup — and you can actually remember it.</li>
      </ul>
    </FailureModes>
  </div>

  <div data-min-level="3" hidden>
    <DeepDive>
      <p>
        Even a well-chosen hash function isn't enough on its own. Two more
        pieces close the remaining gaps:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Salting.</strong> A random value, unique per password,
          stored alongside the hash. Without it, an attacker can
          precompute a <strong>rainbow table</strong> — every common
          password hashed once, reused against every account in every
          breach forever. A salt makes precomputation worthless: the same
          password hashes differently on every account.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Peppering.</strong> A second secret value, shared across
          the whole site but kept <em>outside</em> the database — in a
          secrets manager, not a column. If the database leaks but the
          pepper doesn't, the stolen hashes are useless without it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tunable cost.</strong> Argon2id (the current OWASP
          recommendation) exposes memory, time, and parallelism as
          explicit dials. Turning them up costs the server milliseconds
          per real login — and costs an attacker running billions of
          guesses proportionally more. That asymmetry is the entire point.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </DeepDive>
  </div>

  <WhatILearned>
    You can't control how a site hashes your password. You <strong>can</strong>
    control two things that matter regardless:
    <ul>
      <li>a long, unique passphrase per site</li>
      <li>a password manager to generate and remember the rest</li>
    </ul>
    That combination is what actually survives a breach.
  </WhatILearned>

  <Sources
    sources={[
      {
        title: 'NIST SP 800-63B — Digital Identity Guidelines',
        url: 'https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-3/sp800-63b.html',
        note: 'The actual federal standard behind "length over complexity" — written by the people who used to recommend the opposite.',
      },
      {
        title: 'OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet',
        url: 'https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html',
        note: 'Concrete, current hashing recommendations (Argon2id parameters, bcrypt, scrypt) for anyone actually building the login page.',
      },
      {
        title: 'Have I Been Pwned',
        url: 'https://haveibeenpwned.com/',
        note: 'Check whether your own email or password has already surfaced in a real breach.',
      },
    ]}
  />
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  const input = document.getElementById('pw-input') as HTMLInputElement;
  const lengthEl = document.getElementById('cs-length')!;
  const lowerEl = document.getElementById('cs-lower')!;
  const upperEl = document.getElementById('cs-upper')!;
  const digitEl = document.getElementById('cs-digit')!;
  const symbolEl = document.getElementById('cs-symbol')!;
  const entropyEl = document.getElementById('cs-entropy')!;
  const commonFlag = document.getElementById('common-flag')!;
  const hashRows = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.ht-val'));

  // Widely published "most common passwords" — the same ~100 entries show
  // up across a decade of breach-analysis lists. Not exhaustive; the point
  // is that these get tried before any brute-force math starts.
  const COMMON_PASSWORDS = new Set([
    '123456', 'password', '123456789', '12345678', '12345', 'qwerty', 'abc123', 'password1',
    '111111', '123123', '1234567', 'dragon', 'letmein', 'monkey', 'football', 'iloveyou',
    'admin', 'welcome', 'login', 'princess', 'solo', 'starwars', 'master', 'hello',
    'freedom', 'whatever', 'qazwsx', 'trustno1', 'superman', 'batman', 'shadow', 'michael',
    'jennifer', 'jordan', 'hunter', 'buster', 'harley', 'ranger', 'tigger', 'sunshine',
    'chelsea', 'george', 'andrea', 'joshua', 'maggie', 'mustang', 'baseball', 'dallas',
    'jessica', 'pepper', 'daniel', 'access', 'flower', 'hockey', 'thomas', 'robert',
    'soccer', 'killer', 'andrew', 'charlie', 'secret', 'summer', 'internet', 'service',
    'canada', 'hello123', 'cheese', 'ginger', 'banana', 'biteme', 'matthew', 'corvette',
    'bigdog', 'cowboy', 'boomer', 'yankees', 'lakers', 'test123', 'passw0rd', 'p@ssw0rd',
    'qwerty123', '1q2w3e4r', 'zaq12wsx', 'asdfghjkl', '000000', '1111', '121212', '654321',
    '7777777', '1qaz2wsx', 'iloveyou1', 'myspace1', 'password123', 'welcome1', 'changeme',
  ]);

  function counts(pw: string) {
    let lower = 0, upper = 0, digit = 0, symbol = 0;
    for (const ch of pw) {
      if (/[a-z]/.test(ch)) lower++;
      else if (/[A-Z]/.test(ch)) upper++;
      else if (/[0-9]/.test(ch)) digit++;
      else symbol++;
    }
    return { lower, upper, digit, symbol };
  }

  function charsetSize(c: ReturnType<typeof counts>): number {
    let size = 0;
    if (c.lower) size += 26;
    if (c.upper) size += 26;
    if (c.digit) size += 10;
    if (c.symbol) size += 33;
    return size || 1;
  }

  function formatDuration(seconds: number): string {
    if (!isFinite(seconds) || seconds < 0) return '—';
    if (seconds < 1) return 'instant';
    const units: [string, string, number][] = [
      ['second', 'seconds', 1], ['minute', 'minutes', 60], ['hour', 'hours', 3600],
      ['day', 'days', 86400], ['year', 'years', 31557600],
      ['century', 'centuries', 3155760000], ['millennium', 'millennia', 31557600000],
    ];
    let chosen = units[0];
    for (const u of units) if (seconds >= u[2]) chosen = u;
    const [singular, plural, unitSeconds] = chosen;
    const value = seconds / unitSeconds;
    if (value >= 1e6) return `${value.toExponential(1)} ${plural}`;
    const display = value < 10 ? value.toFixed(1) : Math.round(value).toLocaleString();
    return `${display} ${display === '1' || display === '1.0' ? singular : plural}`;
  }

  function update() {
    const pw = input.value;
    const c = counts(pw);
    lengthEl.textContent = String(pw.length);
    lowerEl.textContent = String(c.lower);
    upperEl.textContent = String(c.upper);
    digitEl.textContent = String(c.digit);
    symbolEl.textContent = String(c.symbol);
    commonFlag.hidden = !pw || !COMMON_PASSWORDS.has(pw.toLowerCase());

    if (!pw) {
      entropyEl.textContent = '0 bits';
      for (const row of hashRows) row.textContent = '—';
      return;
    }

    const bits = pw.length * Math.log2(charsetSize(c));
    const avgGuesses = Math.pow(2, bits) / 2;
    entropyEl.textContent = `${bits.toFixed(1)} bits`;
    for (const row of hashRows) {
      const rate = parseFloat(row.dataset.rate!);
      row.textContent = formatDuration(avgGuesses / rate);
    }
  }

  input.addEventListener('input', update);
  update();

  // Depth toggle: shared across every topic page via the same storage key,
  // so a reader's chosen floor carries over when they move to the next topic.
  const DEPTH_KEY = 'siteDepth';
  const depthButtons = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.depth-btn'));
  const gated = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-min-level]'));

  function applyDepth(level: number) {
    depthButtons.forEach((b) => b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(Number(b.dataset.level) === level)));
    gated.forEach((el) => { el.hidden = Number(el.dataset.minLevel) > level; });
    try { localStorage.setItem(DEPTH_KEY, String(level)); } catch {}
  }

  let initialDepth = 0;
  try {
    const stored = localStorage.getItem(DEPTH_KEY);
    if (stored !== null) initialDepth = Math.min(3, Math.max(0, parseInt(stored, 10) || 0));
  } catch {}
  applyDepth(initialDepth);

  depthButtons.forEach((b) => b.addEventListener('click', () => applyDepth(Number(b.dataset.level))));
</script>

<style>
  .depth-toggle { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--sp-2); margin: var(--sp-4) 0 var(--sp-6); }
  .depth-btn {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--1); letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    padding: var(--sp-2) var(--sp-3); background: var(--bg-1); border: 1px solid var(--line-bright);
    color: var(--text-1); cursor: pointer;
  }
  .depth-btn:hover { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--text-0); }
  .depth-btn[aria-pressed="true"] { background: var(--accent); border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--bg-0); font-weight: 700; }

  .lab { margin: var(--sp-6) 0; padding: var(--sp-4); border: 1px solid var(--line); background: var(--bg-1); }
  .section-heading { margin-bottom: var(--sp-2); }
  .lab-note { color: var(--text-2); font-size: var(--step--1); margin-bottom: var(--sp-4); }
  .pw-label { display: block; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--1); letter-spacing: 0.06em; color: var(--text-1); margin-bottom: var(--sp-2); }
  #pw-input {
    width: 100%; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step-1); padding: var(--sp-3);
    background: var(--bg-0); border: 1px solid var(--line-bright); color: var(--text-0);
  }
  #pw-input:focus { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px; }

  .common-flag {
    margin-top: var(--sp-4); padding: var(--sp-3); background: var(--bg-0);
    border: 1px solid var(--status-broken); color: var(--status-broken);
    font-size: var(--step--1); line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .charset-grid {
    margin-top: var(--sp-4); display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(6, 1fr); gap: 1px;
    background: var(--line); border: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .cs-cell { background: var(--bg-0); padding: var(--sp-2); display: grid; gap: 2px; text-align: center; }
  .cs-key { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.65rem; letter-spacing: 0.06em; color: var(--text-2); text-transform: uppercase; }
  .cs-val { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step-0); font-weight: 700; color: var(--accent); }
  @media (max-width: 40rem) { .charset-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); } }

  .table-scroll { margin-top: var(--sp-5); overflow-x: auto; }
  .hash-table { width: 100%; min-width: 32rem; border-collapse: collapse; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--1); }
  .hash-table th { text-align: left; color: var(--text-2); font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: 0.04em; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.7rem; padding: var(--sp-2) var(--sp-2) var(--sp-1); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-bright); }
  .hash-table td { padding: var(--sp-2); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); color: var(--text-1); }
  .hash-table .ht-val { color: var(--text-0); font-weight: 700; text-align: right; }
  .hash-table tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }
</style>
